A||||16||Grumpy||Buying a 1988 GT||||5||
Z||000000||Grumpy||01-02-2001||06:17 PM||||Hi.<P>Currently I own a 1997 Sunfire GT, 5Spd and I'm strongly considering purchasing a 1988 GT Fiero 5Spd in the spring. I love the idea of a small mid engined, two seater, a real sportscar for the summer months (Store in Winter).<P>Just a few questions if I may.<BR>i) What should I look for when buying a Fiero?<P>ii) What is performance like?... My GT has an estimated 165-170bph at the motor with my current mods.<P>iii) Expensive to maintain?<P>If you like just tell me a bit about your car and why you love it?... Even post pics!<BR>I'd love to see your cars : )<P>I hear they handle very very well.<P>Thanks for any info.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Grumpy<BR>1997 Pontiac Sunfire GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||01-02-2001||06:36 PM||||I think your sunfire should be about the same acelleration-wise. They are both relatively light, the sunfire has more power, and a Fiero will have an aging engine.<P>The Fiero will handle <B>much</B> better than your sunfire. The '88s have a revised suspension, and the difference between an '88 and previous year is notiveable. The '84-'87 still handle exceptionally well though, and can handle better than '88 if modified. You will enjoy a Fiero. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DavidHenshaw||01-02-2001||08:15 PM||||Yeh, I thought I'd park my Fiero for the winter too. Trouble is the thing is so much fun to drive I can't quit. I've compromised by leaving it parked when the roads are bad and firing that baby up between storms. You'll see what I mean.<P>Dave<P>86 GT<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||01-02-2001||08:53 PM||||Summer use cars only.  Fun to drive, parts getting scarce which means costly to repair.  More fun to drive than any car I've owned.  The 88 rides and handles better than the 87, both handle well.  I don't use them for drag races. (see second sentence)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/8788.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000004||Black88GT||01-02-2001||09:05 PM||||There is a buyers guide floating around somewhere, I am sure someone has the link.<BR>I think its on Paul Varygas' website, <A HREF="http://www.xnet.com/~paulv" TARGET=_blank>www.xnet.com/~paulv</A> <P>Basically rust in the trunk area on the sides is a big surprise for most people if they don't look.  Fiero's are like most any car in the sense that if they were previously taken care of, they are reliable, but if you buy one that was beaten on all its life, forget about it.  I know of some good '88 GT's for sale if you are looking for them, email me (fiero5sp@home.com)<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||7
Z||000005||Grumpy||01-03-2001||01:27 AM||||I'm hoping to find the car in the spring. Of course I'm looking for fairly low mileage, and a car that has been taken care of the past 12 years. Garage kept etc.<P>I was going to continue modding my Sunfire GT but I drive it to work everyday so the mileage just piles up. It's probably not worth it in the end, even though it's really fun to mod. Z24's and GT's are really underrated. Pretty quick off the line, 1st and 2nd gear are killer.<P>So I want a nice '88 GT to through in a garage during the week and rip around in it on the weekends. Possibly mod later.<P>Anyway, thanks for your help.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Grumpy<BR>1997 Pontiac Sunfire GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||01-03-2001||01:33 AM||||Grumpy, You said "low mileage" How low and what price range are you looking at? A lot of people back in 88, bought and then stored gt's, thinking that they will be collectors items.  They have not and now many of these cars are up for sale 12 years later. I have bought 2 such cars in the past year.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Grumpy||01-03-2001||07:37 AM||||I was hoping to spend no more than $5000CDN.<BR>I would like a car with around 100,000Khms or less.<P>------------------<BR>Grumpy<BR>1997 Pontiac Sunfire GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||RossT||01-03-2001||07:56 AM||||$5,000 cdn is about $3,500 us<BR>100,000 km is about 62,000 miles<P>you should be able to find a nice 88gt for that kind of money, but you will need to start you search now, and be selective.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||p4n1c||01-03-2001||08:32 AM||||A fully loaded '88 GT is(was) for sale at The Fiero Shop in Toronto, it is(was) going for $9800 and has(had) 90,000kms on it.<P>As you can tell I'm not sure if it's there anymore.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Original email from them:<BR>"At this moment we have an 88 GT auto fully loaded, the car has 90,000 km the asking price is $9800.00 but this is somthing you would have to see and drive."<P><P>------------------<BR>p4n1c<BR>Come out swingin'||||reg||1
